nodecode-langfuse is a rewrite of NousResearch's NousResearch/hermes-official-plugins:

  https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-official-plugins
  commit e7a218f26db88426ab3e7376f0e000b6e356f5e7
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Nous Research (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's langfuse/ folder, whose job was to trace one Hermes
turn as one Langfuse trace, one generation per model call (usage and cost from
Hermes's own pricing) and one observation per tool call, with content capture
sanitized by default. The rewrite keeps that shape on Nodecode's seams: the
:frame point, where every durable fact arrives (turn.usage carries the
provider, the model, the eight token counts and the priced cost; the turn's
terminal fact says the turn is over), and the :tool around hook for a call's
arguments, result, wall time and failure. The langfuse SDK is gone: batches go
to <base_url>/api/public/ingestion as plain JSON over HTTP, with the project's
keys as basic auth, one batch per turn, and a batch the server refuses stays
held for /langfuse send.

Left out, and why: the subagent_* hooks (Nodecode's durable facts do not carry
a subagent span, so a delegated turn traces as its own trace); the end-user
attribution switch (userId) (a tool or frame fact carries no platform sender
id); the SDK's background queue and process-exit flush (a batch is posted when
the turn ends, and /langfuse send is the retry); the read_file head-and-tail
summarizing and the metadata/full per-field raw mode of the original (one
capture mode applies to the whole batch, and truncation says how much it cut);
and the bundled-SDK version pin, since no SDK is bundled.

